<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basilika</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 892</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as τὰ βασιλικά, ta basilika</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Basilika, the imperial laws, was a Byzantine collection of law in sixty books completed around 892 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> by order of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-vi-the-wise">Leo VI the Wise</a>, continuing the work of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a> to simplify and adapt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a> issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> between 529 and 534. Justinian's code was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, by then obsolete in the empire, and many of its laws no longer applied; the Basilika gave the law in Greek, added newer law such as the Procheiron and the Epanagoge, and carried commentaries (scholia). Its influence was limited to the East, but after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a> it was adopted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> until the present Civil Code.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basilika.jpg" alt="Basilika" width="330" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wlbw68 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Василики.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name comes from the Greek adjective meaning imperial laws or enactments, not from the name of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a>, though both words share their root in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basileus">basileus</a>. Between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> and Leo VI the Byzantine legal system had grown confused. The system was codified, so a judge's sentence had to rest on a passage of law already issued by a legislator, and judges could not set precedents. Many were unsure whether to follow Justinian's Codex or the books of law written in the centuries since.</p><p>The project also served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a>. At a time when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> challenged Byzantine leadership, and Louis II mocked a Byzantine emperor's faulty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, the reform helped restate the empire's Roman identity. It also tied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a> and his sons to Justinian and to the age before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iconoclasm">Iconoclasm</a>, lending the dynasty religious legitimacy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The sixty books kept the order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a>, beginning with ecclesiastical law and the sources of law and moving through procedure, private law and administrative law to criminal law. Besides Justinian's texts they took in legal customs that had grown up since, the works begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a> and numerous decrees of the Iconoclast emperors. Because they preserved so many legal documents, they have shaped the study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>.</p><p>The commentaries set the Basilika apart, since Justinian had forbidden commentary on his laws. The scholia came from juristic works of the sixth and seventh centuries, from writers such as Baphius in the tenth or eleventh century, and from the twelfth and thirteenth centuries. The surviving books vary greatly: some exist in a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, with or without scholia, and some are lost entirely.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Basilika” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its 201 articles rested on Roman-Byzantine law and on St. Sava's Nomocanon of 1219; articles 172 and 174, on judicial independence, were taken from the Byzantine <b>Basilika</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
